# Milestone: v0.9.4 (upgraded at W4/v0.10.2-reactive)
# Covers: every() under an explicit tag-prefix scope; Tag.new("name") now
#         creates a UVAL_TAG handle; the canonical cancel-via-tag install
#         half works with a real named tag.
#
# Companion fixtures:
#   tests/chk/reactive/every/basic.chk            — unscoped install + fire.
#   tests/chk/chunk_lifecycle/script_explicit_tag.chk
#                                                 — tag-scoped install +
#                                                   chunk-lifecycle survival.
#
# W4/v0.10.2 upgrade: `var mytag = Tag.new("p")` creates a real UVAL_TAG
# instead of nil.  The brace-block form `mytag: { every (P) X }` still
# allocates an ANONYMOUS scope UTag (OP_PUSH_TAG does not yet bind the
# register's UVAL_TAG as the scope owner — that is W5 bare-prefix).
# At W4, mytag and the scope's anonymous UTag are distinct; calling
# mytag.stop() stops all strands tagged with `mytag` (none — no strand has
# been registered against the UVAL_TAG directly), not the every() periodic.
#
# Remaining gap: the cancel half (`mytag.stop()` stops the periodic) requires
# W5 (bare-label `mytag: every(P) X` without braces, which binds OP_PUSH_TAG
# to use mytag as the scope tag).  When W5 lands, restore:
#   var mytag = Tag.new("p")
#   mytag: every (1us) Realm.fired = Realm.fired + 1
#   mytag.stop()
#   # assert Realm.fired stops advancing.

var fired = 0
var mytag = Tag.new("p")
mytag: { every (1us) Realm.fired = Realm.fired + 1 }
0
0
Realm.fired > 0
[00000000] 0
[00000015] <Tag: p>
[00000015] nil
[00000015] 0
[00000015] 0
[00000015] true
